Dear 3Dnatives team,

Solidon3D 0.3.3 has been released, and there is one detail that may make it
unusually convenient for your editions: the application ships fully localised
in French, English, Spanish and German (plus Italian and Portuguese):
interface, manual and example projects alike. Whichever edition picks the
story up, readers can follow it in their own language.

The core story is a five-minute test: import a downloaded model whose mounting
holes are in the wrong place, click one, and move it, without finding the
source CAD file or rebuilding the part. For a long time only the diameter
could be changed; now a recognised feature can be moved, rotated, resized,
duplicated and removed, and it keeps its identity through the edit, so a fit
defined against it still holds.

What sets it apart from both mesh editors and CAD suites:

* a feature panel showing the measured values (position, diameter, depth,
  axis) as editable fields with a live preview while typing;
* one option that applies an edit to every feature of the same kind, turning a
  row of four holes into a single transaction with a single undo;
* recognition bounded by what a printer can make: on a downloaded hose holder,
  296 of 1130 reported features were noise and are gone;
* an optional AI assistant that drives the same documented operations a click
  does, running locally via Ollama if preferred, never writing geometry
  itself;
* Windows, macOS and Linux packages, no account, no cloud project store.

Download and overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Changelog: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

I can provide a before/after example (source STL, moved and duplicated holes,
printable result) in any of the six languages, and the released build itself -
the complete demo runs free until 30 October 2026, so your editors can verify
every claim hands-on.

